Evaluate desugared models over indexed tables

Desugared models now store each relation in a mutable table instead of
persistent prefix trees. Rows are stamped with their position in the
table's insertion log. A stratum's old rows are those stamped before
its previous read and its new rows are the rest: rules read old and all
rows through hash trie indices, bounding old rows by stamp, and new rows
through sorted copies of the log. Semi-naive evaluation thus needs
neither snapshots nor structural set differences.

Function tables report a second result for the same arguments when a
row is inserted. Evaluation equates such results after every stratum
instead of running generated functionality rules, and the ordinary rules
of a recursive component share one stratum per round. Rules skip rows
that their tables hold already, equal lookups within a routine are
shared, and premises inverting a function choose their plan at run time.

The rule modules of a stratum run as tasks on a small thread pool, each
writing its own output buffers, which are consumed in module order so
that results do not depend on scheduling. While closing, tables defer
trie updates to one parallel batch per trie before each stratum.
EQLOG_THREADS sets the number of threads; 1 evaluates sequentially.

Native models keep the persistent storage and morphism transport. The
ETT checker compiles its kernel as a desugared model, weakens each
scope element once and resolves older elements through the scope chain.
It checks the Nat and Vec developments about 16x faster than before
this branch, and 10x faster with EQLOG_THREADS=1.
